A qualified electronic signature is validated by checking a specific set of conditions and by using a validation system that reports correct results and security issues. In particular:

- The validation process must confirm that all of the following were true at the time of signing:  
  - the supporting certificate was a qualified certificate complying with Annex I (E Art. 32.1(a));  
  - the qualified certificate was issued by a qualified trust service provider and was valid (E Art. 32.1(b));  
  - the signature validation data corresponds to the data provided to the relying party (E Art. 32.1(c));  
  - the unique set of data representing the signatory in the certificate is correctly provided to the relying party (E Art. 32.1(d));  
  - any use of a pseudonym is clearly indicated to the relying party if a pseudonym was used (E Art. 32.1(e));  
  - the electronic signature was created by a qualified electronic signature creation device (E Art. 32.1(f));  
  - the integrity of the signed data has not been compromised (E Art. 32.1(g));  
  - the requirements provided for in Article 26 were met at the time of signing (E Art. 32.1(h)).  
  Note: the content of Article 26 is not provided in the supplied context, so its specific requirements cannot be stated here.

- The validation system must provide the relying party with the correct result of the validation process and allow the relying party to detect any security-relevant issues (E Art. 32.2).

- A qualified validation service may be provided only by a qualified trust service provider that (1) provides validation in compliance with Article 32(1) and (2) allows relying parties to receive the validation result in an automated, reliable, efficient manner, bearing the provider’s advanced electronic signature or seal (E Art. 33.1).

- Recital 57 explains that specifying the components to be assessed by the relying party and the requirements for qualified validation services is intended to ensure legal certainty and make validation easy and convenient at Union level (E Rec. 57).

- A qualified electronic signature based on a qualified certificate issued in one Member State is recognised as a qualified electronic signature in all other Member States (E Art. 25.3).

A qualified electronic signature is validated by a process that confirms the signature is valid only if all of the following are met (E Art. 32.1):
- the certificate supporting the signature was, at the time of signing, a qualified certificate complying with Annex I (E Art. 32.1(a); E Art. 28.1);  
- the qualified certificate was issued by a qualified trust service provider and was valid at the time of signing (E Art. 32.1(b));  
- the signature validation data corresponds to the data provided to the relying party (E Art. 32.1(c));  
- the unique set of data representing the signatory in the certificate is correctly provided to the relying party (E Art. 32.1(d));  
- the use of any pseudonym is clearly indicated to the relying party if a pseudonym was used at the time of signing (E Art. 32.1(e));  
- the electronic signature was created by a qualified electronic signature creation device (E Art. 32.1(f); E Art. 30.1);  
- the integrity of the signed data has not been compromised (E Art. 32.1(g)); and  
- the requirements of Article 26 were met at the time of signing (E Art. 32.1(h)). Article 26(26) requires that an advanced electronic signature be uniquely linked to and capable of identifying the signatory, be created using signature-creation data the signatory can use under his sole control, and be linked to the signed data so any subsequent change is detectable (E Art. 26.26).

Operational and service requirements for validation:
- the validation system must provide the relying party the correct result and allow detection of any security‑relevant issues (E Art. 32.2).  
- the Commission may specify reference standards for validation by implementing acts; compliance with Article 32(1) is presumed where validation meets those standards (E Art. 32.3).  
- a qualified validation service may be offered only by a qualified trust service provider that performs validation in compliance with Article 32(1) and allows relying parties to receive the validation result in an automated, reliable and efficient manner bearing the provider’s advanced electronic signature or seal (E Art. 33.1).

- A qualified trust service provider must verify the identity (and any specific attributes) of the person to whom the qualified certificate is issued, by appropriate means and in accordance with national law; the provider may verify directly or rely on a third party (E Art. 24.1).

- Permitted verification methods are (E Art. 24.1):
  - physical presence of the natural person (or an authorised representative) (E Art. 24.1(a));
  - remote verification using electronic identification means, for which, prior to issuance, a physical presence was ensured and which meets the requirements in Article 8 for assurance level "substantial" or "high" (E Art. 24.1(b); E Art. 8);
  - by means of a certificate of a qualified electronic signature or qualified electronic seal issued in compliance with point (a) or (b) (E Art. 24.1(c));
  - other identification methods recognised at national level that provide assurance equivalent to physical presence, with that equivalent assurance confirmed by a conformity assessment body (E Art. 24.1(d)).

- A qualified trust service provider must verify the identity (and any specific attributes) of the person to whom the qualified certificate is issued, by appropriate means and in accordance with national law; verification may be done directly or by relying on a third party (E Art. 24).  

- Permitted verification methods (E Art. 24):  
  - (a) by the physical presence of the natural person (or an authorised representative of a legal person);  
  - (b) remotely, using electronic identification means, provided that prior to issuance a physical presence was ensured and those electronic identification means meet the assurance levels "substantial" or "high" set out in Article 8;  
  - (c) by means of a certificate of a qualified electronic signature or of a qualified electronic seal issued in compliance with (a) or (b);  
  - (d) by other identification methods recognised at national level that provide equivalent assurance to physical presence (the equivalent assurance must be confirmed by a conformity assessment body).  

- Additional note: for qualified certificates for electronic seals, trust service providers should implement measures to be able to establish the identity of the natural person representing the legal person when such identification is necessary in judicial or administrative proceedings (E Rec. 60).

- A qualified trust service provider must verify the identity (and any specific attributes) of the natural person by appropriate means and in accordance with national law; the provider may verify directly or rely on a third party (E Art. 24).

- Acceptable verification methods are (E Art. 24):
  - physical presence of the natural person (E Art. 24);
  - remote verification using electronic identification means, provided that prior to issuance a physical presence was ensured and those electronic identification means meet the assurance levels "substantial" or "high" set out in Article 8 (E Art. 24; E Art. 8);
  - by means of a certificate of a qualified electronic signature or of a qualified electronic seal issued in compliance with the physical‑presence or remote methods above (E Art. 24);
  - other identification methods recognised at national level that provide equivalent assurance to physical presence (equivalence to be confirmed by a conformity assessment body) (E Art. 24).

- Electronic signatures qualify as "advanced electronic signatures" when they meet all of the following requirements (E Art. 26):
  - they are uniquely linked to the signatory (E Art. 26(a));
  - they are capable of identifying the signatory (E Art. 26(b));
  - they are created using electronic signature creation data that the signatory can, with a high level of confidence, use under his sole control (E Art. 26(c));
  - they are linked to the data signed in such a way that any subsequent change in the data is detectable (E Art. 26(d)).

- Moreover, compliance with those requirements is presumed if the advanced electronic signature meets reference standards established by the Commission by implementing acts (E Art. 27.4).

An electronic signature qualifies as an advanced electronic signature when it satisfies all of the following requirements (E Art. 26):
- it is uniquely linked to the signatory (E Art. 26(a));  
- it is capable of identifying the signatory (E Art. 26(b));  
- it is created using electronic signature creation data that the signatory can, with a high level of confidence, use under his sole control (E Art. 26(c)); and  
- it is linked to the data signed therewith in such a way that any subsequent change in the data is detectable (E Art. 26(d)).

Compliance with those requirements is presumed where an advanced electronic signature meets reference standards established by Commission implementing acts (E Art. 27(4)).

Electronic signatures qualify as advanced electronic signatures when they meet all of the following requirements (E Art. 26):
- (a) they are uniquely linked to the signatory;  
- (b) they are capable of identifying the signatory;  
- (c) they are created using electronic signature creation data that the signatory can, with a high level of confidence, use under his sole control; and  
- (d) they are linked to the data signed therewith in such a way that any subsequent change in the data is detectable. (E Art. 26)

An electronic signature qualifies as an "advanced electronic signature" when it meets all of the following requirements (E Art. 26):
- it is uniquely linked to the signatory (E Art. 26);  
- it is capable of identifying the signatory (E Art. 26);  
- it is created using electronic signature creation data that the signatory can, with a high level of confidence, use under his sole control (E Art. 26); and  
- it is linked to the data signed therewith in such a way that any subsequent change in the data is detectable (E Art. 26).

Compliance with those requirements is presumed where the advanced electronic signature meets standards referenced in implementing acts established by the Commission (E Art. 27.4).

- General rule: a data protection impact assessment (DPIA) must be carried out by the controller where processing operations are likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons (G Rec. 84; G Rec. 90).

- Examples of situations requiring a DPIA (non‑exhaustive) include:
  - large‑scale processing of a considerable amount of personal data at regional, national or supranational level likely to affect a large number of data subjects (G Rec. 91);
  - large‑scale use of a new technology where, given the state of technological knowledge, a high risk is likely (G Rec. 91);
  - processing that makes it more difficult for data subjects to exercise their rights (G Rec. 91);
  - taking decisions about specific natural persons following systematic and extensive evaluation/profiling (G Rec. 91);
  - processing of special categories of personal data, biometric data, or data on criminal convictions and offences (G Rec. 91);
  - monitoring publicly accessible areas on a large scale, especially using optic‑electronic devices (G Rec. 91);
  - any other operations the competent supervisory authority considers likely to result in a high risk (G Rec. 91).

- Timing and procedure:
  - The DPIA must be carried out prior to the processing (G Rec. 90).
  - If a DPIA indicates that, without safeguards, the processing would result in a high residual risk that cannot be mitigated by reasonable means, the supervisory authority should be consulted prior to the start of processing (G Rec. 94).

- Exception:
  - Processing of personal data from patients or clients by an individual physician, other health care professional or lawyer is not to be considered large scale, and in such cases a DPIA should not be mandatory (G Rec. 91).

- Practical note on conduct:
  - The controller shall seek the advice of the data protection officer, where designated, when carrying out a DPIA (G Art. 35.2).

- General rule: a data protection impact assessment (DPIA) must be carried out where processing operations are likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons (G Rec. 84; G Rec. 90).

- Timing: the DPIA should be carried out prior to the processing (G Rec. 90).

- Typical situations where a DPIA is required (non‑exhaustive examples):  
  - large‑scale processing that aims to process a considerable amount of personal data at regional, national or supranational level and could affect a large number of data subjects or is likely to result in a high risk (G Rec. 91);  
  - use of new technology on a large scale or other operations that make it more difficult for data subjects to exercise their rights (G Rec. 91);  
  - processing for decisions regarding specific natural persons following systematic and extensive evaluation of personal aspects based on profiling (G Rec. 91);  
  - processing of special categories of personal data, biometric data, or data on criminal convictions and offences or related security measures (G Rec. 91);  
  - monitoring publicly accessible areas on a large scale, especially using optic‑electronic devices (G Rec. 91);  
  - any other operations which the competent supervisory authority considers likely to result in a high risk (G Rec. 91).

- Exception: processing of personal data by an individual physician, other health care professional or lawyer concerning their patients or clients is not to be considered large scale, and in such cases a DPIA should not be mandatory (G Rec. 91).

- Follow‑up where DPIA shows residual high risk: if a DPIA indicates that, absent safeguards, the processing would result in a high risk that cannot be mitigated by reasonable means, the controller should consult the supervisory authority prior to the start of processing (G Rec. 94).

- Procedural note: where a data protection officer is designated, the controller must seek the DPO’s advice when carrying out the DPIA (G Art. 35.2).

- Selection: The controller must use only processors who provide "sufficient guarantees" to implement appropriate technical and organisational measures so that processing meets the Regulation's requirements and protects data subjects' rights (Art. 28.1; Rec. 81).

- Formal appointment: Processing by a processor must be governed by a contract or other legal act under Union or Member State law, binding the processor to the controller and setting out the subject‑matter and duration of the processing, the nature and purpose of the processing, the type of personal data and categories of data subjects, and the obligations and rights of the controller (Art. 28.3; Rec. 81).

- Mandatory contractual stipulations: The contract or legal act must in particular require that the processor:
  - processes personal data only on documented instructions from the controller (including for transfers), unless required by law (Art. 28.3(a));
  - ensure persons authorised to process data are bound to confidentiality (Art. 28.3(b));
  - take all measures required by Article 32 (security) (Art. 28.3(c));
  - assist the controller in responding to data‑subject rights and in meeting obligations under Articles 32–36, as appropriate (Art. 28.3(e)–(f));
  - at the controller's choice, delete or return all personal data after the end of the provision of services, and delete existing copies unless law requires storage (Art. 28.3(g)); and
  - make available to the controller all information necessary to demonstrate compliance and to contribute to audits, and immediately inform the controller if an instruction infringes the Regulation (Art. 28.3(h)) (Art. 28.3).

- Sub‑processors: If a processor engages another processor, the same data protection obligations must be imposed on the sub‑processor (Art. 28.4).

- Operational constraint: The processor and any person acting under the authority of the controller or processor who has access to personal data shall not process those data except on instructions from the controller, unless required to do so by Union or Member State law (Art. 29.1; Art. 32.4).

- Core requirement: keep a record of processing activities under your responsibility (controller) or carried out on behalf of a controller (processor) (Art. 30.1–2).

- If you are a controller, the record must contain all of the following:
  - name and contact details of the controller and, where applicable, joint controller, controller's representative and data protection officer (Art. 30.1(a));
  - the purposes of the processing (Art. 30.1(b));
  - a description of the categories of data subjects and of the categories of personal data (Art. 30.1(c));
  - the categories of recipients to whom the personal data have been or will be disclosed, including recipients in third countries or international organisations (Art. 30.1(d));
  - where applicable, transfers of personal data to a third country or an international organisation, including identification of that country or organisation and, where applicable, the envisaged time limits for erasure of the different categories of data (Art. 30.1(e)–(f));
  - where possible, a general description of the technical and organisational security measures referred to in Article 32 (Art. 30.1(g); see also Art. 32).

- If you are a processor, the record must contain:
  - name and contact details of the processor(s) and of each controller on behalf of which the processor is acting, and, where applicable, of the controller's or the processor's representative, and the data protection officer (Art. 30.2(a));
  - the categories of processing carried out on behalf of each controller (Art. 30.2(b));
  - where applicable, transfers of personal data to a third country or an international organisation, including identification of that country or organisation and, where possible, a general description of the technical and organisational security measures referred to in Article 32 (Art. 30.2(c); see also Art. 32).

- Availability and purpose:
  - the controller or processor must make the record available to the supervisory authority on request (Art. 30.4);
  - maintaining such records serves to demonstrate compliance with the Regulation (Rec. 82) and supports transparency and appropriate security measures (Rec. 39; Rec. 78; Art. 32).

(If you need a template or examples, that is not provided in the supplied context.)

- Who must keep a record
  - Controllers and, where applicable, their representatives: must maintain a record of processing activities under their responsibility (Art. 30.1).  
  - Processors and, where applicable, their representatives: must maintain a record of all categories of processing activities carried out on behalf of a controller (Art. 30.2).  
  - Such records should be made available to the supervisory authority on request to demonstrate compliance (Rec. 82).

- Minimum contents required for a controller's record (Art. 30.1)
  - name and contact details of the controller and, where applicable, the joint controller, the controller's representative and the data protection officer (Art. 30.1(a));  
  - the purposes of the processing (Art. 30.1(b));  
  - a description of the categories of data subjects and of the categories of personal data (Art. 30.1(c));  
  - the categories of recipients to whom the personal data have been or will be disclosed, including recipients in third countries or international organisations (Art. 30.1(d));  
  - where applicable, transfers of personal data to a third country or an international organisation, including the identification of that third country or international organisation and, in the case of transfers referred to in the second subparagraph of Article 49, where possible, the envisaged time limits for erasure of the different categories of data (Art. 30.1(e));  
  - where possible, a general description of the technical and organisational security measures referred to in Article 32 (Art. 30.1(g)).

- Minimum contents required for a processor's record (Art. 30.2)
  - name and contact details of the processor(s) and of each controller on behalf of which the processor is acting, and, where applicable, of the controller's or the processor's representative, and the data protection officer (Art. 30.2(a));  
  - the categories of processing carried out on behalf of each controller (Art. 30.2(b));  
  - where applicable, transfers of personal data to a third country or an international organisation, including the identification of that third country or international organisation and, in the case of transfers referred to in the second subparagraph of Article 49, where possible, a general description of the technical and organisational security measures referred to in Article 32 (Art. 30.2(c)–(d)).

- Principles to observe when drafting the record
  - Ensure transparency, using clear and plain language about the controller's identity, purposes and other information necessary for fair and transparent processing (Rec. 39).  
  - Include information enabling limitation of storage and periodic review or erasure time limits so data are not kept longer than necessary (Rec. 39).  
  - Reflect technical and organisational measures and internal policies that demonstrate compliance and data‑protection‑by‑design/by‑default principles (Rec. 78).

(Primary legal requirements: Art. 30.1, Art. 30.2; supporting principles and obligations: Rec. 39, Rec. 78, Rec. 82.)

- Purpose: codes of conduct may specify the application of the Regulation (G Art. 40(2)).

- Elements that such a code should/may specify (G Art. 40(2)(a)–(k)):
  - fair and transparent processing (G Art. 40(2)(a));
  - the legitimate interests pursued by controllers in specific contexts (G Art. 40(2)(b));
  - the collection of personal data (G Art. 40(2)(c));
  - the pseudonymisation of personal data (G Art. 40(2)(d));
  - the information provided to the public and to data subjects (G Art. 40(2)(e));
  - the exercise of the rights of data subjects (G Art. 40(2)(f));
  - the information provided to, and the protection of, children, and how parental consent is to be obtained (G Art. 40(2)(g));
  - the measures and procedures referred to in Articles 24 and 25 and the measures to ensure security of processing referred to in Article 32 (G Art. 40(2)(h));
  - the notification of personal data breaches to supervisory authorities and the communication of such breaches to data subjects (G Art. 40(2)(i));
  - the transfer of personal data to third countries or international organisations (G Art. 40(2)(j));
  - out-of-court proceedings and other dispute‑resolution procedures between controllers and data subjects (without prejudice to Articles 77 and 79) (G Art. 40(2)(k)).

- Other relevant points:
  - Codes should take account of the specific features of processing sectors and the specific needs of micro, small and medium‑sized enterprises (G Art. 40(1)).
  - When drawing up, amending or extending a code, associations and bodies should consult relevant stakeholders, including data subjects where feasible, and have regard to submissions and views received (G Rec. 99).

Elements that should be included in a code of conduct:

- Specific topics and rules specifying the application of the Regulation, in particular with regard to:
  - fair and transparent processing;
  - legitimate interests in specific contexts;
  - the collection of personal data;
  - pseudonymisation of personal data;
  - information provided to the public and to data subjects;
  - the exercise of data‑subject rights;
  - information/protection of children and parental consent;
  - measures and procedures referred to in Articles 24 and 25 and security measures referred to in Article 32;
  - notification and communication of personal data breaches;
  - transfers of personal data to third countries or international organisations;
  - out‑of‑court proceedings and other dispute‑resolution procedures for disputes between controllers and data subjects.
  (G Art. 40.2)

- Provision for out‑of‑court dispute resolution and other dispute procedures (G Art. 40.2(k)).

- Consultation of relevant stakeholders, including data subjects where feasible, when drawing up, amending or extending the code (G Rec. 99).

- Calibration of obligations to sector‑specific characteristics and the needs of micro, small and medium enterprises, taking account of the risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons (G Rec. 98).

- Guidance on implementation of appropriate measures and on demonstration of compliance (e.g. risk identification, assessment and best practices to mitigate risk) may be provided by codes of conduct (G Rec. 77).

- Where compliance is monitored by a body in connection with the code, that body should demonstrate independence and expertise, and have procedures to assess eligibility of controllers/processors, to monitor compliance, to handle complaints and to avoid conflicts of interest (G Art. 41.2).

- Purpose and scope: codes should contribute to the proper application of the Regulation and take account of the specific features of processing sectors and the needs of micro, small and medium‑sized enterprises (G Art. 40.1).

- Substantive elements that a code may specify (G Art. 40.2):
  - (a) fair and transparent processing;
  - (b) the legitimate interests pursued by controllers in specific contexts;
  - (c) the collection of personal data;
  - (d) the pseudonymisation of personal data;
  - (e) the information provided to the public and to data subjects;
  - (f) the exercise of the rights of data subjects;
  - (g) the information provided to, and the protection of, children, and how parental consent for children is to be obtained;
  - (h) the measures and procedures referred to in Articles 24 and 25 and the measures to ensure security of processing referred to in Article 32;
  - (i) the notification of personal data breaches to supervisory authorities and the communication of such breaches to data subjects;
  - (j) the transfer of personal data to third countries or international organisations;
  - (k) out‑of‑court proceedings and other dispute‑resolution procedures for resolving disputes between controllers and data subjects, without prejudice to Articles 77 and 79.

- Process element when drawing up or amending a code: associations and other bodies should consult relevant stakeholders, including data subjects where feasible, and have regard to submissions and views received in those consultations (G Rec. 99).

- General scope: Rome II applies to non‑contractual obligations understood as an autonomous concept and its conflict‑of‑law rules cover non‑contractual obligations arising out of strict liability (RII Rec. 11). It also applies to non‑contractual obligations that are likely to arise (RII Art. 2).

- Exclusions: the following non‑contractual obligations are excluded from the scope of the Regulation (RII Art. 1):
  - non‑contractual obligations arising out of family relationships and relationships deemed by the law applicable to such relationships to have comparable effects, including maintenance obligations (RII Art. 1);
  - non‑contractual obligations arising out of matrimonial property regimes, property regimes of relationships deemed comparable to marriage, and wills and succession (RII Art. 1);
  - non‑contractual obligations arising under bills of exchange, cheques and promissory notes and other negotiable instruments insofar as they arise from their negotiable character (RII Art. 1);
  - non‑contractual obligations arising out of the law of companies and other bodies corporate or unincorporated concerning creation, legal capacity, internal organisation, winding‑up, personal liability of officers/members for company obligations, and auditors’ personal liability in statutory audits (RII Art. 1);
  - non‑contractual obligations arising out of relations between settlors, trustees and beneficiaries of a voluntarily created trust (RII Art. 1);
  - non‑contractual obligations arising out of nuclear damage (RII Art. 1);
  - non‑contractual obligations arising out of violations of privacy and rights relating to personality, including defamation (RII Art. 1).

- The Regulation covers non‑contractual obligations as an autonomous concept and expressly covers non‑contractual obligations arising out of strict liability (RII Rec. 11).

- It also applies to non‑contractual obligations that are likely to arise (RII Art. 2).

- However, the following non‑contractual obligations are excluded from its scope (RII Art. 1):
  - (a) non‑contractual obligations arising out of family relationships and relationships deemed by the law applicable to such relationships to have comparable effects including maintenance obligations;
  - (b) non‑contractual obligations arising out of matrimonial property regimes, property regimes of relationships deemed by the law applicable to such relationships to have comparable effects to marriage, and wills and succession;
  - (c) non‑contractual obligations arising under bills of exchange, cheques and promissory notes and other negotiable instruments to the extent that the obligations arise out of their negotiable character;
  - (d) non‑contractual obligations arising out of the law of companies and other bodies corporate or unincorporated regarding matters such as creation, legal capacity, internal organisation or winding‑up, and personal liability of officers/members or auditors in statutory audits;
  - (e) non‑contractual obligations arising out of the relations between settlors, trustees and beneficiaries of a trust created voluntarily;
  - (f) non‑contractual obligations arising out of nuclear damage;
  - (g) non‑contractual obligations arising out of violations of privacy and rights relating to personality, including defamation (RII Art. 1).

- The provided text does not state a standalone or explicitly named "right to explanation." (G Rec. 39)

- The Regulation does, however, grant data subjects rights to transparency and information about processing, including:
  - a right of access to personal data and to be informed of the purposes, recipients, and the logic involved in any automatic personal data processing, and, at least when based on profiling, the consequences of such processing (G Rec. 63);
  - an obligation on the controller to inform the data subject of the existence of profiling and the consequences of such profiling (G Rec. 60);
  - general transparency duties and the right to obtain confirmation and communication of personal data being processed (G Rec. 39);
  - modalities to facilitate exercise of those rights (access, rectification, erasure) and an obligation to respond without undue delay and at the latest within one month (G Rec. 59).

- Conclusion: The context supplied does not describe a separate "right to explanation" term, but it does require that controllers provide information about the logic and consequences of automated processing and profiling and to facilitate access to relevant information (G Rec. 63; G Rec. 60; G Rec. 39; G Rec. 59).
